Remember the Core Principles of Business Reporting
• Demystify the Jargon: Avoid heavy corporate acronyms (such as EBITDA or ROI without context) and explain technical financial terms using plain, everyday language.
• Provide Deeper Analysis: Move beyond the surface-level "what" to explain why an event matters, what comes next, and how it impacts the broader national or global economy.
• Maintain Neutral Sourcing: Interview neutral, independent experts, academics, and regulatory bodies alongside corporate or labor representatives—and actively guard against PR spin or propaganda.
• Use the Inverted Pyramid Structure: Lead with the most vital, newsworthy facts in the opening paragraph (the "lede"), followed by supporting data and historical context.
• Ground Data in Human Impact: Connect macro-level figures (like GDP, inflation, or interest rates) to everyday realities, showing how policy changes and market shifts affect real people

